# A clean file can still crash a submitted job with "null bytes"

Symptom: a Slurm job (or any process on a shared/NFS-backed filesystem)
crashes instantly with something like:

```
File "/path/to/main.py", line N
SyntaxError: source code cannot contain null bytes
```

...but reading the exact same file directly (from the login node, or even
via `ssh` to the compute node) shows zero null bytes and parses fine with
`ast.parse`. This happened once with `main.py` after a long editing session
with multiple environment resets in between.

This is a stale filesystem-level cache tied to the file's **old inode**
(check `stat file.py` — the inode will look unremarkable, but a lower-level
cache layer on the compute node or its NFS client is still serving old
blocks). Editing the file in place does not fix it, and clearing
`__pycache__` does not fix it either (that's the wrong layer).

This has recurred more than once, on different files (`main.py` first, then
`training/config.py` on 2026-08-02) — the common thread each time is a file
edited once and then not touched by any subsequent job submission for a
day or more before a fresh compute-node job tries to read it. Treat any
first-launch-after-a-quiet-period `SyntaxError: ... null bytes` as this
issue by default and apply the fix immediately, rather than assuming it is
file-specific.

## The same root cause has other symptoms besides literal null bytes

Confirmed 2026-08-07: a large-scale incident (89 candidates false-failed
across two live searches over more than 24 hours, `main.py`/
`model/matrix_qwen.py`/`scripts/eval/turn_taking_probe.py` all affected on
different compute nodes at different times) showed this same stale-NFS-cache
root cause manifesting as THREE different symptoms, not just the literal
"null bytes" message:

- `SyntaxError: '(' was never closed` (or other nonsensical syntax errors)
  at a line that is valid, correctly-closed code in the real file --
  i.e. genuinely corrupted/truncated bytes served by a stale cache, just a
  different corruption pattern than a literal null byte run.
- `AttributeError: 'Namespace' object has no attribute 'some_removed_field'`
  (or similarly "the current source could never produce this") where the
  traceback's displayed source line (via `linecache`, which reads the LIVE
  file) does not match what the error implies was actually executing --
  this is stale **compiled bytecode** (a `__pycache__/*.pyc` whose embedded
  source mtime/hash still matches what the compute node's NFS client
  believes is the source file's current mtime, so Python trusts the cache
  and never recompiles) rather than corrupted raw bytes. `grep` the
  suspected removed/changed attribute name across the ENTIRE current
  codebase first -- if it doesn't exist anywhere, the running code cannot
  possibly be current source, confirming this diagnosis before treating it
  as a real bug.
- The problem is genuinely **node-specific and time-dependent, not global**:
  the exact same file/inode ran correctly (including in this incident's own
  smoke tests) on some compute nodes/time windows while failing on others
  (e.g. H100 nodes were fine while a specific V100 node was not) -- a
  successful smoke test on ONE node is not proof a file is safe everywhere;
  don't stop investigating just because your own recent test passed.
- Restarting a long-lived worker pool (to deploy an unrelated code fix, e.g.
  a `worker.py` bugfix) can itself be what exposes a LATENT stale-cache
  problem on a DIFFERENT file, by causing Slurm to reschedule onto a node
  whose NFS client has a different (stale) cache state than the job it
  replaced. Don't assume a fresh restart's failures are caused by the thing
  you just changed -- check whether the actual traceback is from a
  completely unrelated file first.
- **Processed data and metadata can be affected too, not only Python
  source.** Confirmed 2026-08-10: a previously healthy lookback-64
  `manifest.json` became binary garbage (invalid UTF-8 and null bytes), and
  reading the AMI Arrow shard returned impossible 64-bit `length` values
  before the Python process segfaulted. A training process that had already
  memory-mapped/loaded the dataset continued normally, so "current jobs are
  healthy" did not prove the on-disk dataset remained reusable for a fresh
  job. Before any final evaluation contract or new long-lived run, validate
  the manifest as UTF-8 JSON and iterate every Arrow row checking
  `length == len(input_ids[0])` plus expected matrix shapes. Do not attempt
  to repair a corrupted Arrow shard by rewriting metadata derived from the
  corrupted read.

## Fix

1. `Delete` the file, then `Write` it back with the exact same content
   (verified against your last-known-good version first). This forces a
   brand-new inode.
2. Also delete any matching `__pycache__/<module>.cpython-*.pyc` next to it
   locally (defensive -- doesn't fix the remote NFS cache by itself, but
   removes one more place stale bytecode could be hiding).
3. Verify the new inode: `stat file.py` before/after should show a
   different `Inode:` number.
4. Re-run tests / re-submit the job to confirm the fix actually worked
   before moving on — don't assume it's fixed just because the rewrite
   succeeded locally.
5. **Check the blast radius before moving on.** If a search/worker pool has
   been running against the broken file for a while, inspect its FULL
   candidate failure history (not just whether jobs are currently RUNNING)
   for the same error signature across every affected search directory --
   this bug tends to hit multiple files and multiple searches in the same
   window, not just the one you happened to notice first. Any candidate
   failure caused by this must be retried (never counted as a real
   hyperparameter result), and if it was already told to the search's
   Optuna study as a false zero, treat it per
   `.cursor/rules/search-worker-handled-failures.mdc` -- a small number of
   early/random-phase false zeros can be left as a documented, contained
   limitation, but if a large fraction of a study's real (non-import)
   trials are contaminated, rebuild that study fresh (archive the old
   `optuna_study.db`, clear `optuna_trial_number`/`optuna_told` on every
   candidate without a genuinely valid score, backfill only from real
   scores) rather than leaving a study that has no real signal left in it.
6. For corrupted processed data, rebuild to a **new directory/inodes** from
   raw source, validate every row and a real evaluation-contract build, then
   preserve the old directory under a `.corrupted_<date>` name and
   atomically rename the validated rebuild into the canonical path. Existing
   processes with open mappings may continue, while new processes resolve
   the repaired canonical path.
